The Rhylthyme MCP tools are unavailable in this offline evaluation, so I cannot run `search_public_recipes`, `validate_program`, `analyze_schedule`, or `visualize_schedule` or produce a live URL. Below is the completed relationship model and the full program JSON built to be self-consistent with the rules.

**Tracks and trigger decisions:** I used one `operator` track for all hand tasks so that the one person’s work is serialized, and separate instrument tracks for the thermocycler, microcentrifuge, microwave, gel cooling/setting, gel rig, and transilluminator so that machine-only work can run while the operator is idle or doing a different hand task. The main parallelism is PCR cycling while the agarose is microwaved, cooled, poured, and set. The loading step is gated by a compound join because it requires three readiness conditions: gel in rig, ladder ready, and PCR products dyed.

**Improved question:**  
“Beyond the numbered instructions, which resource occupancies and readiness waits are hidden by phrases like ‘twenty-five minutes into the cycling run,’ ‘cool for 12 minutes,’ ‘let the gel set,’ ‘ten minutes after the gel is poured to set,’ ‘when the cycling finishes,’ and ‘nothing goes into a well until’?”

**Answer:**  
The work is not just a linear list. It has hidden joins and holds: the operator can start the gel only after cycling has started and then reaches 25 minutes; the gel cannot be poured until the agarose has cooled; the gel cannot be transferred until it has set; the ladder cannot be prepared until 10 minutes after pouring; the PCR products cannot be dyed until cycling finishes; and loading cannot begin until the gel is in the rig, the ladder is ready, and the products are dyed. The eight clones are batched in shared operations, so I do not replicate steps or create eight tracks; the scale is captured in metadata. The revised triggers encode these waits as `afterStep` offsets, `afterStepWithBuffer`, and `compound` joins.

